\chapter{\texorpdfstring{\texttt{FixedString}: Compile-Time Strings as
Template
Parameters}{: Compile-Time Strings as Template Parameters}}\label{ch4-fixedstring-compile-time-strings-as-template-parameters}

MuDSL is built around a single recurring idea: the names, tags,
patterns, and rule identifiers that shape a domain-specific language
should be known at compile time. A grammar rule called \texttt{"add"}, a
token tag called \texttt{"num"}, and a regular-expression pattern like
\texttt{"[0-9]+"} are not runtime configuration values but structural
facts about the program. Carrying those facts through the type system
lets the compiler optimize, check, and dispatch on them without any
runtime cost.

The obstacle, of course, is that C++ has historically refused to accept
a string literal as a non-type template parameter (NTTP). This chapter
introduces \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString}, the
small literal class type that removes that obstacle. Every downstream
chapter that writes something inside angle brackets ---
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}"[0-9]+"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}"\_\allowbreak{}km"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}"name"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}when\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and the PEG
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- depends on the type defined here.

\section{The Problem: Strings as Template
Parameters}\label{ch4-the-problem-strings-as-template-parameters}

Before C++20, the non-type template parameter space was narrow. A
template could be parameterized by integral constants, enumerators,
pointers, and references to objects with static storage duration. A
string literal, however, is an array of \texttt{const char} with
automatic storage duration, and arrays decay to pointers in most
contexts. The obvious attempt fails to compile.

\begin{code}
template <const char *Tag> struct leaf;   // declaration

leaf<"num"> n;                             // error before C++20
\end{code}

The compiler rejects this because \texttt{"num"} does not designate an
object with static storage duration in the sense the standard requires
for a pointer NTTP. Even when a workaround using a reference to an array
was employed, each distinct tag still required a separate named variable
declared at namespace scope, which defeated the goal of writing
self-documenting inline tags.

The consequence for a library like MuDSL would be severe. Instead of
writing
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}lhs,\allowbreak{} rhs)\allowbreak{}},
a user would have to declare
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} char add\_\allowbreak{}tag[] = "add";}
somewhere and then write
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}add\_\allowbreak{}tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}lhs,\allowbreak{} rhs)\allowbreak{}}.
The tag would no longer live at the point of use, grammar definitions
would sprawl across declarations, and the pedagogical clarity that MuDSL
is designed to deliver would evaporate. A type-level solution is needed.

The same obstacle blocks more than convenience. Many of MuDSL's
compile-time mechanisms --- pattern dispatch in
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}, rule
naming in the rewrite system, production naming in the PEG layer ---
rely on the compiler being able to distinguish one string from another
at instantiation time. Without a string NTTP, each of those mechanisms
would have to fall back on runtime comparison of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} values, which forfeits
inlining, type-level dispatch, and the guarantee that two grammatically
identical tags denote the same specialization. The library's design
depends on the tag being part of the type, not part of the data.

\section{C++20 Class-Type Non-Type Template
Parameters}\label{ch4-c20-class-type-non-type-template-parameters}

C++20 relaxed the NTTP rules in a targeted way. A literal class type may
now be used as a non-type template parameter provided that all of its
non-static data members are of structural types, none are of reference
type, and the class meets the usual literal-type requirements.
Structural types include scalar types, lvalue reference types, and
arrays of structural types. A class containing a plain \texttt{char}
array therefore qualifies.

This is the door that \texttt{FixedString} walks through. Because it is
a literal class type whose only non-static data member is a
\texttt{char} array, it is a valid NTTP. Each distinct string literal
produces a distinct
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}N\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
value, and the compiler treats two \texttt{FixedString} values with the
same contents and length as the same template argument. The library can
finally write
\texttt{leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and mean it.

The structural-type rule has a subtle but important consequence. Two
template arguments of class type are considered to designate the same
template parameter when their corresponding members have the same
values. For \texttt{FixedString}, that means the bytes of \texttt{data}
must match element by element. This is exactly the semantics a tag type
should have: two nodes tagged \texttt{"num"} are interchangeable
regardless of where they were constructed.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}N\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Definition}{The  Definition}}\label{ch4-the-fixedstringn-definition}

The type is deliberately minimal. The entire definition fits in ten
lines and depends only on
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}cstddef\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} for
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}size\_\allowbreak{}t},
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}algorithm\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
for \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}copy\_\allowbreak{}n}, and
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
for the \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} return type. Quoted
verbatim from \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}:

\begin{code}
template <std::size_t N> struct FixedString
{
  char data[N]{};
  constexpr FixedString (const char (&s)[N]) { std::copy_n (s, N, data); }
  constexpr std::string_view
  view () const
  {
    return { data, N - 1 };
  }
  constexpr bool operator== (const FixedString &) const = default;
};
\end{code}

Every member is \texttt{constexpr}, every operation is trivially
copyable, and the type has no virtual functions, no base classes, and no
reference members. The design is intentionally compatible with being
stored in the compiler's internal representation of a template argument.

The single template parameter \texttt{N} is the length of the string
including its null terminator. A
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}4\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
therefore holds three visible characters plus a trailing
\texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'}. This convention matches the declared type
of a string literal in C++, which is \texttt{const char[N+1]} for an
N-character literal, and it is what allows the constructor to deduce
\texttt{N} directly from a literal argument.

The empty string is a valid special case. A
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}1\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
carries only the terminator and represents a zero-length view. It arises
naturally in the library whenever a rule or suffix is intentionally left
blank, and the structural-equality rules treat all such instances as the
same specialization. No special-casing is required in the definition to
support it.

\section{Storage and the Null
Terminator}\label{ch4-storage-and-the-null-terminator}

The data member \texttt{char data[N]\{\}} is value-initialized, so every
byte starts at zero. When the constructor runs, it copies all \texttt{N}
bytes from the source array, terminator included, into \texttt{data}.
The trailing null is therefore always present, which makes \texttt{data}
safe to pass to any C API that expects a NUL-terminated string in
addition to being usable through
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

The null terminator is not merely decorative. Because
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} of
length \texttt{N - 1}, the visible string never includes the terminator;
but the raw \texttt{data} array still ends in
\texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'}, so \texttt{data} can be used directly
where a C-style string is required. This dual nature --- a C string when
you need one, a sized view when you do not --- is a small but persistent
convenience throughout the library.

The fact that \texttt{N} is part of the type is the central design
trade-off. A
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}4\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and a
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}5\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are unrelated types as far as the type system is concerned, even if both
happen to spell \texttt{"num"} when padded. In practice this is not a
limitation, because every literal in a program has a single, fixed
length known to the compiler, and the deduction in the constructor picks
the correct \texttt{N} automatically.

\section{The Constructor and Array-Reference
Deduction}\label{ch4-the-constructor-and-array-reference-deduction}

The constructor's parameter type,
\texttt{const char (\allowbreak{}\&s)\allowbreak{}[N]}, is a reference
to an array of exactly \texttt{N} \texttt{const char}. This is the idiom
that makes string-literal deduction work. When the constructor is called
with \texttt{"abc"}, the literal has type \texttt{const char[4]}, so
\texttt{N} is deduced as 4 and the constructor receives a reference to
that exact array. No decay to \texttt{const char*} occurs, and no length
information is lost.

\begin{code}
constexpr dsl::FixedString a = "abc";   // N deduced as 4
static_assert(a.view() == "abc");
static_assert(a.view().size() == 3);
\end{code}

The body copies all \texttt{N} bytes with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}copy\_\allowbreak{}n(\allowbreak{}s,\allowbreak{} N,\allowbreak{} data)\allowbreak{}}.
Because \texttt{N} includes the terminator, the copy carries the
trailing \texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'} into \texttt{data} as well.
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}copy\_\allowbreak{}n} is itself
\texttt{constexpr} in C++20, so the whole constructor is usable during
constant evaluation.

The use of \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}copy\_\allowbreak{}n},
rather than a hand-rolled loop, is a deliberate readability choice. The
standard algorithm is recognized by optimizers and produces the same
code as a \texttt{memcpy} on trivially copyable types, while remaining
explicitly \texttt{constexpr}. The constructor body therefore stays a
single statement, which keeps the definition short enough to inspect at
a glance.

Because the parameter is a reference to an array of fixed size, the
constructor rejects arguments whose size does not match the deduced
\texttt{N}. This is not a runtime check; it is a compile-time
consequence of the parameter type. Passing a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} or a \texttt{const char*}
simply does not match the constructor signature, which prevents
accidental loss of length
information.\texttt{cpp dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString b(\allowbreak{}"hello")\allowbreak{};            /\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} N deduced as 6 \allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString c(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string(\allowbreak{}"x")\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}; /\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} error:\allowbreak{} no matching con\allowbreak{}structor}

The constructor is not \texttt{explicit}, which allows the implicit
conversion that takes place when a string literal is supplied as a
template argument. Writing
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}42)\allowbreak{}}
relies on the compiler implicitly constructing a \texttt{FixedString}
from \texttt{"num"} at the template-argument position. This implicit
conversion is the ergonomic keystone of the whole library.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
Method}{The  Method}}\label{ch4-the-view-method}

The \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
spanning the first \texttt{N - 1} characters of \texttt{data}. The
length is one less than the array size precisely because the null
terminator is excluded from the logical string. The returned view is a
lightweight handle --- a pointer and a length --- and incurs no
allocation.

\begin{code}
constexpr dsl::FixedString tag = "identifier";
constexpr std::string_view v = tag.view();
static_assert(v.size() == 10);
static_assert(v == "identifier");
\end{code}

The method is declared \texttt{constexpr} so that it can be evaluated
during constant expression. This matters because the library uses
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} inside
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}} initializers, inside
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}, and inside the bodies of
\texttt{constexpr} functions such as \texttt{leaf} and \texttt{node}.
Without \texttt{constexpr}, the tag could not propagate into the type
system at compile time.

The \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
return type was chosen over
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} deliberately. Returning a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} would require dynamic
allocation, which is not permitted during constant evaluation and would
defeat the zero-overhead goal. A \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}, by
contrast, is trivially copyable and points directly into the
\texttt{data} array stored inside the \texttt{FixedString} object, which
lives as long as the template argument that holds it.

A consequence of this design is that the
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} returned by
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is only valid for as long as
the \texttt{FixedString} from which it came. In MuDSL this is never a
problem, because the \texttt{FixedString} is a template argument and
therefore lives for the duration of the program. Users who copy a
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} result into a local
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} variable should keep this lifetime in
mind, but in ordinary usage the view is consumed immediately.

It is worth emphasizing that \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
never allocates and never throws. The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
constructor it invokes is trivial, and \texttt{data} is always a valid,
NUL-terminated array. This makes
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} safe to call from
\texttt{noexcept} contexts and from code paths that must remain
allocation-free, such as the hot paths of the matcher and the AST
dumper.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The Defaulted
\texttt{operator==}}{The Defaulted }}\label{ch4-the-defaulted-operator}

The final member is a defaulted equality operator. C++20's defaulted
comparisons perform memberwise comparison, so two \texttt{FixedString}
objects compare equal if and only if every element of \texttt{data}
matches. Because \texttt{N} is part of the type, comparisons only occur
between objects of the same length, and the comparison reduces to a
simple byte-by-byte test over the array.

\begin{code}
constexpr dsl::FixedString x = "add";
constexpr dsl::FixedString y = "add";
constexpr dsl::FixedString z = "sub";
static_assert(x == y);
static_assert(!(x == z));
\end{code}

The \texttt{default} keyword ensures that the operator is both
\texttt{constexpr} and usable during constant evaluation. It also makes
the comparison strong in the C++20 sense: there is no need to write a
custom body, and the compiler is free to optimize the array comparison
as it sees fit. The structural-equality semantics required for NTTPs
align exactly with the semantics of this defaulted operator, so two
\texttt{FixedString} template arguments that compare equal also
designate the same template specialization.

Because equality is \texttt{constexpr}, tags can be compared at compile
time inside \texttt{if constexpr}, inside
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}, and inside any \texttt{consteval}
context. This is what enables the
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
dispatch machinery described in Chapter 8 to select among branches at
compile time: each branch's key is a \texttt{FixedString} (possibly
wrapped in a \texttt{pattern}), and the selection is a
constant-expression comparison.

\section{\texorpdfstring{All-\texttt{constexpr}
Design}{All- Design}}\label{ch4-all-constexpr-design}

Every operation \texttt{FixedString} provides --- construction,
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and equality --- is
\texttt{constexpr}. The type is therefore usable in any
constant-expression context, including the initialization of
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}} data members, the arguments of
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}, the bodies of \texttt{consteval}
functions, and, crucially, the evaluation of template arguments. This
uniformity is not an accident; it is the property that makes the type
usable as an NTTP with runtime-accessible values.

\begin{code}
template <dsl::FixedString S> struct tag_of
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();
};

using add_tag = tag_of<"add">;
static_assert(add_tag::value == "add");
\end{code}

The fact that the bytes of a \texttt{FixedString} template argument are
readable at runtime, even though the argument itself is fixed at compile
time, is what bridges the compile-time and runtime worlds. A function
instantiated with
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can call \texttt{Tag.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} at
runtime and obtain a perfectly ordinary
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} that
spells \texttt{"add"}. This is how
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
ends up labelling its \texttt{ASTNode} with a runtime string: the tag is
a compile-time fact, but the label is read out at runtime through
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.

This bridge is one-directional. A compile-time \texttt{FixedString} can
be projected into a runtime \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}, but a
runtime \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} cannot be lifted back into a
\texttt{FixedString} NTTP. MuDSL respects this asymmetry throughout:
tags flow outward into runtime strings for display and matching, but
never inward from runtime data into the type system. Recognizing this
boundary is the key to using the library without fighting the language.

\section{\texorpdfstring{How \texttt{FixedString} Flows Into the
Library}{How  Flows Into the Library}}\label{ch4-how-fixedstring-flows-into-the-library}

\texttt{FixedString} is not a standalone utility; it is the connective
tissue of the entire library. Almost every chapter that follows uses it
in some form. The pattern is always the same: a string literal appears
inside angle brackets, the compiler implicitly constructs a
\texttt{FixedString} from it, and the resulting NTTP parameterizes a
class or function template.

The most direct uses are \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf}
and \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node}, which build the leaf
and inner nodes of an AST. Their signatures, quoted from
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}, show the \texttt{FixedString Tag}
parameter in the leading position.

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
ASTNode leaf (T &&val);

template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
  requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
ASTNode node (Children &&...children);
\end{code}

Inside \texttt{leaf}, the tag is read out at runtime with
\texttt{Tag.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and stored as
the \texttt{ASTNode}'s label. The value is streamed into a string and
stored as the node's payload. The tag never becomes a runtime variable
until \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is called; before that,
it is purely a compile-time fact carried in the type.

\begin{code}
auto n = dsl::leaf<"token">("identifier");
// n.tag() == "token", n payload == "identifier"
\end{code}

The \texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template, used by the compile-time regex engine described in Chapter 9,
takes its regular-expression source as a \texttt{FixedString}. The
signature is
\texttt{template \textless{}\allowbreak{}FixedString S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} struct pattern},
and the body stores a
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view value = S.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The pattern string is therefore baked into the type at compile time and
available to the matcher without any runtime construction.

\begin{code}
template <dsl::FixedString S> struct pattern
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();
  // ... matching machinery ...
};
\end{code}

The custom-literal machinery in Chapter 17 uses \texttt{FixedString} to
name the suffix of a literal. The \texttt{lit} factory and its
\texttt{LiteralEntry} carrier parameterize on
\texttt{FixedString Suff\allowbreak{}ix}.

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Suffix, typename F> struct LiteralEntry
{
  F handler;
  static constexpr std::string_view suffix = Suffix.view ();
  // ...
};

template <FixedString Suffix, typename F>
constexpr auto lit (F &&f);
\end{code}

A usage such as
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}"\_\allowbreak{}km"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}[](\allowbreak{}long double v)\allowbreak{}\{ return v * 100\allowbreak{}0.\allowbreak{}0L; \})\allowbreak{}}
binds the suffix \texttt{"\_\allowbreak{}km"} into the type at compile
time, so that the parser can recognise the suffix without any runtime
lookup table.

The rewrite system in Chapter 13 names its rules with
\texttt{FixedString}. A
\texttt{RewriteRule\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag,\allowbreak{} Pred,\allowbreak{} Trans\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
carries the rule name as a compile-time string, allowing rewrite sets to
be addressed and composed by name. The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}"name"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
factory (Chapter 13 and Chapter 14) follows the same pattern.

\begin{code}
template <FixedString Tag, typename Pred, typename Trans>
struct RewriteRule;
\end{code}

The \texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
dispatch clauses of Chapter 8 use \texttt{FixedString} indirectly
through
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
but they also accept integral and enumerator keys. When a \texttt{when}
clause is keyed on a \texttt{pattern}, the underlying
\texttt{FixedString} is what carries the regular-expression text into
the type system.

\begin{code}
dsl::match(input,
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">>([](auto){ return "number"; }),
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">>([](auto){ return "word"; }),
  dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "other"; })
);
\end{code}

Finally, the PEG grammar layer of Chapter 27 and Chapter 28 uses
\texttt{FixedString} to name productions. The
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
member and the
\texttt{ProductionHandle\allowbreak{}r\textless{}\allowbreak{}Id,\allowbreak{} Fn\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
carrier both key on a \texttt{FixedString}, so an entire PEG grammar can
be spelled out in compile-time strings without a single runtime
identifier table.

\section{Building Tagged Nodes}\label{ch4-building-tagged-nodes}

The most common use of \texttt{FixedString} is labelling AST nodes.
Chapter 11 covers \texttt{leaf} and \texttt{node} in depth; here we
focus on the role of the tag. The tag is supplied as a string literal in
angle brackets, and the compiler converts it to a \texttt{FixedString}
before the template is instantiated.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main ()
{
  auto x = dsl::leaf<"x">(1);
  auto y = dsl::leaf<"y">(2);
  auto add = dsl::node<"add">(x, y);
  std::cout << add.tag() << " " << add.dump() << "\n";
  // prints: add (add (x 1) (y 2))
}
\end{code}

The tag \texttt{"add"} is a compile-time fact about the \texttt{node}
call. It cannot be changed at runtime, it cannot be supplied from a
config file, and it cannot be built from user input. This rigidity is
intentional: the structure of the tree is part of the program's design,
not part of its runtime data.

Because the tag is part of the type, two \texttt{node} calls with
different tags instantiate different specializations of the
\texttt{node} template. The compiler is free to inline each
specialization independently, and there is no virtual dispatch or
string-table lookup involved in constructing a node. The only runtime
work is the \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ostringstream}
formatting of the value and the construction of the \texttt{ASTNode}
itself.

The example program from
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}02-fixed-strings\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}cpp}
exercises this path end to end with only a handful of lines.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto n = dsl::leaf<"token">("identifier");
  auto t = dsl::node<"pair">(n, dsl::leaf<"kind">("name"));
  std::cout << t.tag() << " " << t.dump() << "\n";
}
\end{code}

Here \texttt{"token"} and \texttt{"kind"} are leaf tags, and
\texttt{"pair"} is an inner-node tag. Each is a distinct
\texttt{FixedString} NTTP, and each flows through to the
\texttt{ASTNode}'s label via \texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
The output is
\texttt{pair (\allowbreak{}pair (\allowbreak{}token identifier\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} (\allowbreak{}kind name)\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
demonstrating that the compile-time tags have become ordinary runtime
strings by the time the tree is dumped.

\section{Comparing Tags}\label{ch4-comparing-tags}

Because \texttt{operator==} is defaulted and \texttt{constexpr}, tags
can be compared at compile time. This is useful whenever the structure
of a tree is to be inspected by type rather than by value. A simple
predicate over a node's tag can be written as a \texttt{constexpr}
function.

\begin{code}
template <dsl::FixedString Tag>
constexpr bool is_add (std::string_view t)
{
  return t == Tag.view ();
}

static_assert(is_add<"add">("add"));
static_assert(!is_add<"add">("sub"));
\end{code}

Two tags of the same length compare equal only when every byte matches,
which is exactly the structural-equality rule the language applies to
class-type NTTPs. This means that
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}add\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and a hypothetical second instantiation
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}add\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are the same specialization, and the compiler will not emit duplicate
code for them.

Runtime comparison works just as well. The defaulted \texttt{operator==}
is callable on ordinary runtime objects, so a function that receives two
\texttt{FixedString} values (for example, by storing them in a
container) can compare them with the usual \texttt{==} syntax.

\begin{code}
bool same (dsl::FixedString<4> a, dsl::FixedString<4> b)
{
  return a == b;
}
\end{code}

Note that the two arguments must have the same \texttt{N}, because
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}4\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}5\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are distinct types. When comparing tags of potentially different
lengths, the correct approach is to compare their
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} results, since
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}operator==}
handles differing lengths correctly.

The distinction between structural equality on the type and string
equality on the view matters in two places. Inside the type system, two
\texttt{FixedString} arguments are the same template argument only when
their bytes match exactly, so a
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}"ab"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and a
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}"ab\textbackslash{}0"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(length three plus terminator) would be distinct specializations even
though their visible text is the same. At runtime, by contrast,
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} collapses such distinctions
because it compares only the visible characters. Most library code
compares views, which is the more forgiving of the two.

\begin{code}
template <auto A, auto B>
constexpr bool same_tag ()
{
  return A.view() == B.view();
}

static_assert(same_tag<dsl::FixedString{"abc"}, dsl::FixedString{"abc"}>());
static_assert(!same_tag<dsl::FixedString{"abc"}, dsl::FixedString{"abcd"}>());
\end{code}

\section{Tags as Dispatch Keys}\label{ch4-tags-as-dispatch-keys}

The \texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
machinery described in Chapter 8 uses \texttt{FixedString}-backed keys
to build a compile-time dispatch table. Each \texttt{when} clause
carries its key as a template argument, and the \texttt{match}
implementation walks the clauses in order, comparing the input against
each key. When the key is a
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the comparison is a regular-expression match; when the key is a
\texttt{FixedString}-convertible value, the comparison is structural.

This pattern replaces the runtime \texttt{switch}-on-string idiom that
C++ does not support natively. Instead of a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}unordered\_\allowbreak{}map\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the dispatch table is encoded directly in the type system, and the
compiler is free to inline each branch.

\begin{code}
auto label = dsl::match("add",
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"add">>([](auto){ return "plus"; }),
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"sub">>([](auto){ return "minus"; }),
  dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "unknown"; })
);
\end{code}

The tags \texttt{"add"} and \texttt{"sub"} are \texttt{FixedString}
values embedded in the \texttt{pattern} specializations. They never
appear as runtime strings in the dispatch machinery itself; only their
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} results do, and only at the
point where the matcher actually compares them against the input.

\section{A Tagged-Union-of-Values
Demo}\label{ch4-a-tagged-union-of-values-demo}

A small but complete demonstration shows how \texttt{FixedString} tags
can drive a tagged-union style of value. Rather than declaring an
enumeration and a variant, the tags are written directly into the type
of each constructor. A visitor can then dispatch on the tag using
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main ()
{
  auto make_num = [](int v)  { return dsl::leaf<"num">(v); };
  auto make_str = [](std::string s) { return dsl::leaf<"str">(s); };

  auto v1 = make_num(42);
  auto v2 = make_str("hello");

  auto describe = [](auto const &node) {
    return dsl::match(node.tag(),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"num">>([&](auto){ return "a number"; }),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"str">>([&](auto){ return "a string"; }),
      dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "something else"; })
    );
  };

  std::cout << describe(v1) << "\n";  // a number
  std::cout << describe(v2) << "\n";  // a string
}
\end{code}

The tags \texttt{"num"} and \texttt{"str"} are compile-time facts about
the two \texttt{leaf} calls. The \texttt{describe} function recovers
them at runtime via
\texttt{node.\allowbreak{}tag(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, which
returns the
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} that
\texttt{leaf} stored from
\texttt{Tag.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} clauses
then match against that view using the patterns, which are themselves
\texttt{FixedString}-backed.

This is the central pattern of MuDSL: compile-time names flow through
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} into runtime strings, and
runtime strings are matched back against compile-time names.
\texttt{FixedString} is the hinge that makes the round trip possible.

\section{Limitations}\label{ch4-limitations}

The first limitation is that \texttt{N} is part of the type. A
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}4\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and a
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}5\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
are unrelated types, and there is no common base that holds a tag of
arbitrary length. In practice this is invisible, because the constructor
deduces \texttt{N} from the literal and the user never writes the length
explicitly. It does mean, however, that a function cannot accept ``any
\texttt{FixedString}'' without making \texttt{N} a template parameter of
its own.

\begin{code}
template <std::size_t N>
void process (dsl::FixedString<N> tag);   // accepts any length
\end{code}

The second limitation is that the bytes are copied into the type. A
\texttt{FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}100\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
carries one hundred bytes in its \texttt{data} array, and every copy of
the value copies those bytes. For the short tags typical of a grammar
this is negligible, but it does mean that \texttt{FixedString} is not a
good vehicle for large compile-time blobs. The type is designed for
identifiers, not for payloads.

The third limitation is fundamental to the NTTP mechanism: the string
must be a literal known at compile time. A \texttt{FixedString} cannot
be constructed from a \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string},
from user input, or from any value that is not a constant expression.
This is not a deficiency of \texttt{FixedString}; it is the defining
property of a non-type template parameter. Runtime-built strings must be
carried by other means, such as the \texttt{ASTNode}'s label or the
payload of a \texttt{leaf}.

\begin{code}
std::string user_tag = read_from_config();
// dsl::leaf<user_tag>(42);   // error: user_tag is not a constant expression
\end{code}

The fourth limitation follows from the structural-equality rule: two
tags compare equal as NTTPs only if their bytes match exactly, including
the terminator. Padding or trailing spaces will produce distinct
template specializations. Users should therefore write tags as bare
literals without extraneous whitespace.

\section{Relationship to Standard
Proposals}\label{ch4-relationship-to-standard-proposals}

The C++ standardization committee has, over several cycles, considered a
\texttt{fixed\_\allowbreak{}string} type that would formalize the
pattern represented here by \texttt{FixedString}. The most visible
appearance of the idea in production code is
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}format}'s use of a fixed-string
type to carry format strings as compile-time constants, enabling
compile-time validation of format specifiers. \texttt{FixedString} is a
minimal, self-contained realization of the same idea, tuned for the
specific needs of an embedded-DSL library.

The standard proposals differ from \texttt{FixedString} in detail ---
they may support trailing capacity, constexpr iteration, or integration
with \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} --- but the core
principle is identical: a literal class type with a \texttt{char} array,
usable as an NTTP, with \texttt{constexpr} access to its contents.
Should a standardized \texttt{fixed\_\allowbreak{}string} arrive, it
would be a drop-in replacement for \texttt{FixedString} at the
template-argument position, and the rest of MuDSL would be unaffected.

Until then, \texttt{FixedString} provides the same service with a
ten-line definition and no dependencies beyond the standard library
headers that \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} already includes. Its
smallness is a feature: it makes the type easy to read, easy to reason
about, and easy to verify against the structural-type rules of C++20.

\section{Summary}\label{ch4-summary}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString\textless{}\allowbreak{}N\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a literal class type whose single non-static data member is a
\texttt{char} array of size \texttt{N}. Its constructor deduces
\texttt{N} from a string literal via a reference-to-array parameter and
copies the literal, terminator included, into the array with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}copy\_\allowbreak{}n}. The
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} of
length \texttt{N - 1}, and a defaulted \texttt{operator==} provides
structural equality. Every member is \texttt{constexpr}, so the type is
usable in any constant-expression context.

Because \texttt{FixedString} is a literal class type with structural
members, it qualifies as a C++20 class-type non-type template parameter.
This is what allows string literals to appear inside angle brackets
throughout MuDSL:
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"num"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}"add"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}"[0-9]+"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}lit\textless{}\allowbreak{}"\_\allowbreak{}km"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}"name"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the keys of
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}, and
the
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
calls of the PEG layer all depend on this property. The tag is a
compile-time fact carried in the type; its
\texttt{view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is the runtime string
recovered from it.

The type carries the usual limitations of an NTTP carrier: \texttt{N} is
part of the type, the bytes are copied into the type, and only string
literals --- not runtime strings --- may be used as arguments. Within
those limits, \texttt{FixedString} is the mechanism that lets MuDSL
spell a grammar in source code rather than in configuration. The
remaining chapters of this manual use it freely, and this chapter is the
foundation for all of them.
